Massive Haemorrhage (Terminal Event)
Palliative & End of Life
Sudden catastrophic blood loss, Immediate profound shock, Extremely rapid deterioration

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: PEL-039
Reason for Visit: Sudden, catastrophic exsanguinating hemorrhage from the oral cavity resulting in profound shock and impending death.
Location: Inpatient Hospice Facility
Present at Visit: Patient, hospice registered nurse, distressed spouse
Urgency Justification: The hospice nurse initiated an emergency code for a sudden, overwhelming, and catastrophic loss of a massive volume of bright red blood from the patient's mouth. The patient immediately progressed into profound hypovolemic shock with an absent peripheral pulse and unarousable coma. Immediate medical presence was absolutely required to confirm the terminal nature of the event, administer massive doses of crisis sedation/analgesia to ensure absolute comfort, and provide urgent trauma support to the spouse and staff during this horrific dying process.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: Hospice RN states: "The carotid tumor ruptured. He started coughing up massive amounts of bright red blood two minutes ago and instantly collapsed. He is completely unresponsive."
History of Present Illness: The patient has a known, advanced head and neck malignancy with tumor encasement of the right carotid artery. Moments ago, there was a sudden, overwhelming, and catastrophic loss of a massive volume of bright red blood. This rupture originated directly from the locally invasive malignant tumor eroding the major vascular structure. The patient suffered profound, immediate hypovolemic shock, manifesting as an absent peripheral pulse and unrecordable blood pressure. There was a severe, rapid onset of altered mental status immediately progressing directly to an unarousable coma. The catastrophic massive hemoptysis was accompanied by extreme, profound cutaneous pallor and instantly cold, visibly clammy peripheral extremities. The nurse noted a brief period of severe, agonizing subjective air hunger and rapid gasping explicitly secondary directly to sudden, profound systemic hypoxemia, before the patient progressed to agonal respirations. The extremely rapid deterioration explicitly led directly to impending cardiac arrest.
Functional Status: Unconscious, agonal state.
Social/Safety Concerns: Extreme, profound psychological trauma for the spouse witnessing the catastrophic blood loss; severe biohazard scene requiring immediate management.

