The diagnosis is absolute and visual. No differential diagnosis is required or appropriate given the catastrophic, exsanguinating nature of the witnessed carotid blowout in a known head and neck cancer patient.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Critical

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

This is a catastrophic, unsurvivable terminal event. The patient has suffered a massive, exsanguinating hemorrhage due to tumor erosion into a major artery. The objective findings of profound coma, unrecordable blood pressure, and agonal respirations confirm the patient is in the final minutes of life. Attempting any life-prolonging interventions, such as CPR or transferring to an emergency department, would be medically futile, explicitly contravene the Advanced Care Directive, and inflict horrific, unnecessary trauma on the patient and family. Immediate intervention was exclusively focused on administering stat massive doses of parenteral sedatives to absolutely guarantee the patient is experiencing zero terror or air hunger, utilizing dark towels to mask the visual trauma of the blood, and providing intense, immediate psychological shielding for the spouse.

